pth it can be hard to answer  these questions so when you're reading  through the documentations  sometimes these tiny tiny details  stick out so that's all those tools  together they  they come together and that was really  what i did to  prepare for the security exam nice okay  yeah sounds like a mix of  um you know learning about the different  services  through a cloud guru database  documentation and also  through practice tests and yeah i think  that's a really good approach  so my next question is and this is  something that a lot of the viewers  always ask about how difficult is the  aws security exam  um relative to the other exams so you  know the  cloud practitioner associate solutions  architect and even the professional ones  i think specialty exams are very very  interesting compared to the other side  of things that  i i'm assuming that most of the viewers  are associate or cloud prac thinking of  going specialty  those types of questions the associate  types questions  they tend to ask general knowledge  on a lot of services now this differs to  the security specialty because  in security specialty there's only a  couple  of the top of my head might be wrong  five or six  key services in aws that  when you go into the specialty exam or  into the security exam  these are kind of what the questions are  focused around so you won't necessarily  switch between services  in associate but you would rather be  asked  deeper like very very deep questions  continually about the same question now  that ties into the the concept of  difficulty because  in the associate if you have a general  knowledge on a lot of services you can  quite easily go comfortable into that  exam and get a pretty good score  and with the specialty however you need  to really drill down  on these key services so personally i  don't think  they're hard i just think that um  especially for the security one i think  it's a great lead on if you've got all  the associates you'll actually find that  in those in the practice for those exams  you have already been preparing yourself  for security so i think it's a good  leap way or a good stepping stone if  you're thinking of going for a specialty  but it's not impossible if you learn  these services  80 to 90 as to what they can do  then you can quite easily go into this  exam comfortable and  getting a higher score so it's a deeper  but narrow scope for these exams that  makes a lot of sense so for the  associate ones you're kind of expected  to know about  so many different services but not  really in detail for all of them  but whereas you know security specialty  and the other special  certifications they'll focus on a  specific set of services  and you'll be required to know a bit  more in detail and  speaking of these services right for  security specialty which  services do you focus on the most and  which ones do you  think came up the most during your  practice exams and during the exam just  anything you felt like um students can  really focus on  when preparing for the exam so i think  now thinking off the top i'm thinking of  all the services it might relate to  the dominant and this just sticks out to  me just because  um encryption is a big deal or in terms  of security encryption is kind of like  the first angle people look at  if you really want to kick start for the  security exam  become very familiar with kms  and cloud hsn those two services if i'm  remembering from my security exam  they came up a lot another topic that  came up a lot was  not necessarily networking but the  security  of networks so looking into stuff like  knackles  security groups waff if you want to  get a good start in security i think if  you focus on  encryption so kms and cloud hsl and then  combine that with the networking side of  things so looking into vpc's  security groups that will give you a  good bulk  of the security exam and then the rest  you just naturally have to learn  about them and yeah that's what i would  say yeah  that's very helpful and for my upcoming  security exam as well  i'll keep those services in mind so  patrick my final question  is what challenges did you face when you  were studying for the security specialty  i know one thing we mentioned just  offline was a lot of the time in forums  we see that  students tend to overestimate the  difficulty of the exam  because sometimes you know you think  that you need a couple of years of  experience in a certain domain so is  that something  we should talk about as well to the  viewers so i'll take  two angles on this question so the first  angle is the  challenging part of it and now at the  start i mentioned one of the techniques  i used when i studied for exams  was that after i've done the quizzes and  hack guru  i then go take those services it doesn't  have to be all of them but just the  services that i know  go in depth like kms i take them to the  aws documentation  and then going page by page or tab by  tab  for some this doesn't work but for me it  works because  when you read these documentations they  reveal those types of  questions that might come up in the exam  but you don't necessarily learn  from watching a video you know like when  we talk about kms it might be a couple  of videos about the key features  but because it's a specialty exam there  is going to be some questions  that are going to be very very one-liner  the type of questions where  you see it once and then if you don't  really think about it for a while you  might forget about it  but i would recommend from the  challenging side like get familiar with  the questions  that aren't main features the types of  questions  that are hidden behind the main page  but are still an option now the other  side of it which you did mention with  the forums  specialty exams when for me i'm going to  speak  when i finished all my associates i was  looking at you know what was the next  exam  i could go for is it a pro or could i  ta